Graduate Numerical Methods Applied to Chemical Engineering — MIT 10.34 (@ic-mit-numerics: 0, is #reference, #part-of @ic-sources) { r_1NTDP6EFQGR8HWP34VT9ZJ98DV
William H. Green, Jr. and James W. Swan. Numerical Methods Applied to Chemical Engineering, MIT 10.34, Fall 2015, graduate level. [Public course](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-34-numerical-methods-applied-to-chemical-engineering-fall-2015/) · [Lecture notes](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-34-numerical-methods-applied-to-chemical-engineering-fall-2015/pages/lecture-notes/) · [Programming work](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-34-numerical-methods-applied-to-chemical-engineering-fall-2015/pages/assignments/).

Complete the published numerical/programming progression: linear/nonlinear systems, differential equations, transport/reaction computations and molecular-model problems. Reimplement central results independently and use the shared reproducible-computing methods to test conditioning, convergence, conservation, uncertainty and changed environments. Programming preparation is followed through the relevant shared material where needed.

Course identity, instructors, graduate level, topic coverage and published resource types were checked on 29 October 2026. OCW's CC BY-NC-SA and component terms apply; dated software examples require an explicitly recorded environment. The external course code/media are referenced rather than installed, mirrored, or claimed to have been run.

